questions.smallest_change_that_unblocks:
Two changes, both hours not weeks, neither requiring a platform decision.

(1) Assert the resolver chain in a unit test and remove the unused resolvers outright rather than relying on ordering:

```csharp
Configure<AbpTenantResolveOptions>(options =>
{
    options.TenantResolvers.RemoveAll(r =>
        r.Name is QueryStringTenantResolveContributor.ContributorName
               or RouteTenantResolveContributor.ContributorName
               or HeaderTenantResolveContributor.ContributorName
               or CookieTenantResolveContributor.ContributorName);
    options.TenantResolvers.Insert(0, new HostAwareDomainTenantResolveContributor(...));
});
```
Removal (not just reordering) also closes the empty-Host abstention path, where `Request.Host.HasValue == false` causes the domain contributor to return without setting `Handled` and the chain continues. Pin it with the five-line assertion in I11 so a future ABP upgrade re-adding defaults fails the build rather than silently re-opening the path.

(2) Add a `default_server` block on 443 that returns 421 for any unrecognised Host, and refuse `admin.*` on the public listener. This is a handful of nginx lines and it simultaneously closes the apex fall-through to the AuthServer, the `api.<base>` fall-through to the Angular container, and the OWASP private-virtual-host exposure of the host-scope `admin` slug.

Do these before the platform-selection exercise, because both are properties of the application and the edge config rather than of any provider, and (1) in particular is a live cross-tenant data-access path.

